      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess the feasibility and efficacy of microwave ablation (MWA) of painful refractory bone and soft tissue tumors performed under local anesthesia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "A retrospective study between 2011 and 2013."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "A single center, Academic Interventional Pain Management Unit."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS",
            "content": "Fifteen patients with 25 refractory painful bone (N\u2009=\u200919) or soft tissue (N\u2009=\u20096) tumors treated with MWA were consecutively included."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "Local Institutional Review Board approval was obtained, and written informed consent was waived. Lesions included spinal (N\u2009=\u20093), sacral (N\u2009=\u20094), and extraspinal (N\u2009=\u200918) locations. Pain was measured on a visual analog scale (VAS) from 0 to 10 before and immediately after procedure, at 1 week, and on a monthly basis following procedure. MWA procedures were always performed under computed tomography guidance and local anesthesia along with nitrous oxide inhalation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mean ablation time was 4.09 minutes (range 1-11) with an average of 4.2 cycles with a mean ablation power of 60\u2009W. Preprocedure mean VAS score was 7.2\u2009±\u20090.97 (range 6-9). Follow-up postprocedure VAS scores were as follows: day 0: 1.64\u2009±\u20091.86, day 7: 1.82\u2009±\u20091.79, month 1: 2.05\u2009±\u20092.03 (14/15 patients), month 3: 2.13\u2009±\u20091.81, month 6: 2.36\u2009±\u20092.17; and were statistically significant (P\u2009<\u20090.001). Mean pain relief was 5.5 months."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "MWA is feasible, safe, and effective in the management of painful refractory bone and soft tissue tumors. It may therefore be considered as a potential alternative to existing percutaneous ablation techniques in the management of bone and soft tissue tumors."
          }
        ]
      },
